  "description": "Part Change of Use from Community Hall to Guest Rooms & Accommodation\n\nBackground\nThe Association of Roman Catholic Hungarians in Great Britain (referred later as ‘Society’) was formed 70 years ago, and registered with the FCA in 1955 as a Benevolent Society under the Friendly Societies Act 1974.\nThe Society was established for the charitable purposes of:\na) Supporting the Chaplain and the mission of the Hungarian Roman Catholic Chaplaincy;\nb) Organising community and cultural events which will advance members’ and their guests’ knowledge of the Hungarian art, music, literature and history;\nc) Provide assistance for Hungarians in need, experiencing difficulties.\nFor the first 53 years, the Society’s centre was located in Notting Hill. In 2007 the Management Committee recognised that the centre was not suitable for the Society to fulfill its mission. Sold the building and purchased a larger property in South Ealing at 62 Little Ealing Lane, London. \nThis used to be the primary school building of Mount Carmel Catholic Primary School. The new community centre under the name of St Stephen House, was opened in 2007.\nOver the last 17 years the Society and Management Committee were serving the Hungarian community on many levels, through religious services and gatherings, community meetings, fundraising events, etc. Although the Society’s mission is to look after the Hungarian community here in England and the UK, it also caters for other events, has already hosted the local Northfield Ward Forum a couple of times in the past as well as gives space for the Ealing Bridge Club and their meetings on a regular basis.\n\n The centre currently has a hall where the main activities are being held. As being the main base for the Roman Catholic Chaplaincy (working in partnership with the Westminster Diocese), most of the religious activities and meeting are being held here in the community centre. The Chaplain (priest) lives in the building, he also has his offices and there is a consecrated chapel on the first floor where services are being held on a regular basis. There are other community rooms in the building where we aim to provide a range of educational, cultural, recreational and social opportunities which strengthen social links and breakdown barriers within and between communities. \n\nAn improvement plan was put forward to change the centre’s status to a ‘mixed-use community centre’.\nLetting the rooms provides much needed income for the non profit association which provides a valuable asset not just for the Hungarian community but also the local indigenous catholic community. \n",
